<span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“The… the lock was loose.”</span> <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> says softly, <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“I used to see him smack it with his bear until it opened. Then he’d used the chair you used to sit on when reading to get onto the dresser and climb into mine. I… I think so anyway. I… dream about that sometimes.”</span> <br>
<br>
<span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Heh… such a little troublemaker.”</span> His mother chuckles, before taking a deep breath. You see her reach out and grab something from her bag… the Owlman Bear from <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Connor</span>’s room. She stares down at it as she talks, her voice trembling, <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“It was… two months after your third birthday party. <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Connor</span> had been crying a lot recently, especially at night. He… he wasn’t eating that much either. We took him to a few doctors, but… but they just said babies cry. Allergies, gas, who could really tell why?”</span> <span style="Color: LightCoral">You feel your heart sink as you realize what story she is telling. <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> realizes it too, staring at the gravestone. He looks… scared</span> <span style="Color: LightGreen">but determined. He wants to know what happened, even if it hurts. He needs to know.</span> <br>
<br>

<<linkreplace"Hold his hand">>
    <span style="Color: Pink">You reach out and grab <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span>’s hand. You’ll be by his side as he hears this, to give him the support he needs so he can hear… what he needs to hear.</span> <br>
    <br>
    <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“I didn’t sleep much those days; I used to sit by his crib, trying to soothe him to sleep. It worked, at first, but… it got worse. Every night he just cried… and cried… until he stopped crying.”</span> Her voice is shaky, but she takes a deep breath and continues, <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“I remember the city lights just… flying by. Your father drove so fast, he used his sirens, sped, everything to just… just get there a few seconds faster. We left you and <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Callum</span> with the neighbors until your grandmother could come down. <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Connor</span> was calm and sleepy but could tell something was wrong. But I… I remember you crying as we left.”</span> <br>
    <br>
    <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“He was… so still as we ran him inside. I was screaming and crying and just… begging for someone to help. Even at 3 years old the two of you were so… small…”</span> She pauses, voice trembling, <span style="Color: Pink">but <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> squeezes her hand, turning to her.</span> <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“They told us to wait, but I just kept asking and asking and asking… And I prayed that we weren’t too late. That he would…”</span> <br>
    <br>
    … <br>
    <br>
    … <br>
    <br>
    <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Then the doctor came out and… he didn’t know how to… It was just four words. Said so simply…”</span> <br>
    <br>
    <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Your child is…”</span> <span style="Color: LightCoral">She can’t finish her sentence, instead dropping the bear and suddenly embracing <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> in a tight, desperate hug. She holds him tightly, both of them having tears streaming from their faces.</span> <br>
    <br>
    <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“How?”</span> <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> gets out one word. He has to know. <br>
    <br>
    <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Your brother died from an intestinal blockage.”</span> She replies, <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“I… I know I couldn’t tell you that before. You… you asked where he was the first time I saw you after he… A few months after… I was diagnosed as bipolar with delusional episodes, or ‘psychotic features’ as the doctor put it so elegantly.”</span> She pulls back from <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> but looks at him directly in the eyes. <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“No more secrets, no more just waiting for things to get better. I know this disease affects all of you just as much as it affects me, even if it’s different.”</span> <br>
    <br>
    <span style="Color: Pink">She then reaches into the bag and pulls out <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span>’s Hero Award. It has been glued together, rather chaotically, but it’s in one piece again.</span> <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“I… realized what I did when I came back out to the kitchen and you were gone and your dad was trying to pick this up off the floor. I’m… I’m so sorry I couldn’t see you for how wonderful you are.”</span> <span style="Color: Pink">Her voice finally breaks completely,</span> <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“I’m sorry for being so afraid I couldn’t acknowledge your success, your wonderful deeds. You are the most amazing kid, you bring so much light into the world. I’m so sorry I tried to dimmish it. I was afraid your light would make it impossible to see your brother’s shadow. I thought… if I let you shine to brightly, somehow, I’d lose what little of <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Connor</span> I had left, but all I did was cast his shadow over you. You’re <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span>, my son. Not just <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Connor</span>’s twin, not just the one that survived, not just a reminder of him. You’re my son, and I love you…”</span> <br>
    <br>
    <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“And I’m sorry I couldn’t always see you like that.”</span> She hands the glued award to <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span>, who had just been staring at his mother, tears flowing as he’s overcome with emotion. <span style="Color: Pink">He looks down at the award for only a second, before putting it on the grass and hugging his mom. She has no idea how much <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> needed to hear that, or maybe she did, and that’s why she knew she had to come here, to the place most painful for her.</span> <br>

